  "seoBody": "<h2>How much does Fulcrum cost?</h2><p>Fulcrum publishes Professional at 43 US dollars per user licence a month and Elite at 55 US dollars. Their pricing page states that all plans require an annual subscription and a minimum of five users. The smallest real commitment is therefore five licences for a year, whether or not five people inspect. Elite is where ArcGIS Enterprise connectivity, API and webhook access, audit logging and SSO sit, so check which tier carries the feature you are buying for.</p><h2>Why look for a Fulcrum alternative?</h2><p>Two recurring reasons, neither of which is product weakness. The annual five-seat minimum is a real commitment for a firm with three or four inspectors. And Fulcrum is built to produce structured, mappable records rather than the report your firm issues on its own letterhead, so if a client expects your document, somebody rebuilds it at a desk.</p><h2>What is Fulcrum better at?</h2><p>Geospatial work, clearly. Automatic geotagging, line and polygon capture, offline maps and proper ArcGIS Enterprise connectivity, alongside excellent offline capture with photo, audio, video, barcode and signature. For utilities, environmental survey or asset inventory across a territory, Fulcrum is the stronger tool and Quickler does not compete with it on that ground.</p><h2>What does Quickler charge instead?</h2><p>£50 per phone number a month with a £50 monthly minimum, billed monthly in arrears by UK Direct Debit. No annual commitment and no seat block. A phone number counts when it did at least one check that month, so people who do nothing that month cost nothing. The free tier is 10 reports a month, free forever, with no card.</p>",
